  "question": {
    "text": "Let $f: R \\rightarrow R$ be twice continuously differentiable. Let $f(0)=f(1)=f^{\\prime}(0)=0 .$ Then,",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "fHXXx",
      "text": "$f^{\\prime \\prime}(x) \\neq 0$ for all $x$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "oghxu",
      "text": "$f^{\\prime \\prime}(c)=0$ for some $c \\in R$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "68ULh",
      "text": "$f^{\\prime \\prime}(x) \\neq 0$ if $x \\neq 0$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "DSuJ8",
      "text": "$f^{\\prime}(x)>0$ for all $x$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let function $f(x)=x^{2}(x-1)$ \r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad f^{\\prime}(x)=3 x^{2}-2 x$\r\nand $\\quad f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)=6 x-2$\r\n$\\begin{array}{ll}\\text { Now, } & f(0)=f(1)=f^{\\prime}(0)=0\\end{array}$\r\nThen, according to question, at $x=\\frac{1}{3}, \\quad f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)=0$\r\n(i.e. $c=1 / 3$ for some $C \\in R$ )",
    "image": null
